  "body": "With the ability to create child Web Workers, it is possible to create a \"tree\" of workers. If two of these nodes want to communicate point-to-point, a `MessageChannel` can be created in one Worker and one of the `MessagePort`s can be transferred to the other. Currently, this involves (AFAIK) having to know the \"up and down\" paths in the hierarchy, and doing a series of port transfers through the path.\r\n\r\nSince a Worker can be named, a direct transfer of ports could be implemented using this identifier. This could be implemented on either the `MessageChannel` or `Messageport`.\r\n\r\n```\r\n const worker1 = new Worker(constructor1, {name: \"worker1\"} );\r\n const worker2 = new Worker(constructor2, {name: \"worker2\"} );\r\n\r\n// In worker1\r\nconst channel1 = new MessageChannel();\r\n\r\nchannel1.Port1.Transfer(\"worker2\");\r\n```\r\n\r\nThis would send a message to worker2, carrying the transferred port, with semantics in the message identifying the transferring worker/window.\r\n\r\n",
